            <ArticleTitle>Antibodies to Bordetella pertussis antigens in maternal and cord blood pairs: a Thai cohort study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e4043</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7717/peerj.4043</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Background" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Pertussis is a vaccine-preventable disease, yet an increasing incidence of pertussis occurs in many countries. Thailand has a long-standing pertussis vaccination policy, therefore most expectant mothers today had received vaccines as children. The resurgence of pertussis among Thai infants in recent years led us to examine the pre-existing antibodies to Bordetella pertussis antigens in a cohort of 90 pregnant women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We evaluated the IgG to the Pertussis toxin (PT), filamentous hemagglutinin (FHA) and pertactin (PRN) in maternal and cord blood sera using commercial enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays (ELISA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">When values of &gt;10 IU/ml were accepted as potential protective concentrations, we found that the percentages of unprotected infants were 73.3%, 43.3% and 75.5% for anti-PT, anti-FHA and anti-PRN IgG, respectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Discussion" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">These results may explain the susceptibility for pertussis among newborn infants in Thailand and support the requirement for a pertussis booster vaccine during pregnancy, which may contribute to the passive seroprotection among newborns during the first months of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
